\f0\b\fs24 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone \outl0\strokewidth-167 \strokec2 CS 630 Activity 8-2 Detailed Notes for Students
\f1\b0\fs22 \outl0\strokewidth-182 \
Technical Instructions\
\
This week\'92s assignment is to take a sample python program with a test and build continuous integration around it, specifically using Jenkins. The files you need are in the ~/CS630/8-2 directory in codio. You can also find a public repository to fork in GitHub at:\
	https://github.com/heusserm/CS630\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs36 \cf2 \outl0\strokewidth-112 Test Programs\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs22 \cf2 \outl0\strokewidth-182 \
The program is written in python; you will be testing the API. The code library is triangle.py; the actual API is stand_up_triangle_api.py which sets up the API running on port 5082. test program is test_stood_up_triangle.py. To run the test program, you\'92d do something like this:\
\
In one tab:\
	cd ~/CS630/8-2\
	python3 stand_up_triangle_api.py\
\
In another:\
	cd ~/CS630/8-2\
	pytest\
\
Go back to the first tab and press control-c to cancel the API.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs36 \cf2 \outl0\strokewidth-112 Starting Jenkins\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs22 \cf2 \outl0\strokewidth-182 \
Jenkins is installed in your codio. To start it, go to the terminal, and type this:\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs24 \cf2 \outl0\strokewidth-167 sudo systemctl start jenkins\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4 \cf3 \cb3 \outl0\strokewidth-167 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs22 \cf2 \cb1 \outl0\strokewidth-182 That command will launch Jenkins in the background. Wait a few seconds. Then, to check the status of your launched Jenkins, type:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f5\fs24 \cf2 \outl0\strokewidth-167 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3 \cf2 \outl0\strokewidth-167 sudo systemctl status jenkins\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f5 \cf2 \outl0\strokewidth-167 \
If Jenkins is running, it will be a server on your own computer (localhost). To find Jenkins, launch a web browser and direct it to: \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://localhost:8080/"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \outl0\strokewidth-167 http://localhost:8080}}\
\
When you launch Jenkins, it will ask you for a code. You can find that code by running this command on the terminal:\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3 \cf2 \outl0\strokewidth-167 sudo cat /var/lib/jenkins/secrets/initialAdminPassword \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f5 \cf2 \outl0\strokewidth-167  \
That will give you a complex codeword. You can highlight it and right click and copy it into your paste buffer, then paste it into a browser.\
\
After that, you can create an admin user or just skip it, using username admin and the password from terminal.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4 \cf3 \cb3 \outl0\strokewidth-167 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs22 \cf2 \cb1 \outl0\strokewidth-182 There are at least two ways you could configure Jenkins: \
\
1) You could store the files in git in a public repository, then configure git to run a new build every time.\
\
2) You could configure GIT to run periodically (every 15 minutes? Hour?) and, perhaps, check if any files have changed on the file system. This can be done with the FSTrigger Plugin. ( {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://plugins.jenkins.io/fstrigger/"}}{\fldrslt \ul \ulc4 \outl0\strokewidth-182 https://plugins.jenkins.io/fstrigger/}} )\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs36 \cf2 \outl0\strokewidth-112 What to turn in\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs22 \cf2 \outl0\strokewidth-182 \
Jenkins does not have a simple way to export configuration, so you\'92ll want to create a word document that demonstrates your Jenkins build is configured correctly. A few ways to do this:\
\
\pard\tx20\tx360\pardeftab720\li360\fi-360\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\fs20\fsmilli10000 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1)	}
\fs22 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone \outl0\strokewidth-182 \strokec2 Print out the configuration for the job you created, which you can find in /var/lib/jenkins/<JOB_NAME>/config.xml\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
2)   Cut and paste the console output from Jenkins of a successful, and perhaps, a failing build.\
\
3)   Include screen captures of your job, build history, a specific build, etc.\
\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs36 \cf2 \outl0\strokewidth-112 References\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs22 \cf2 \outl0\strokewidth-182 \
The Jenkins Documentation on installing and running Jenkins on Linux may be helpful:\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.jenkins.io/doc/book/installing/linux/"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul \ulc4 \outl0\strokewidth-182 https://www.jenkins.io/doc/book/installing/linux/}} note that Jenkins will be pre-installed on codio.\
\
You may also benefit from this medium page on how to run python in Jenkins:\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://medium.com/@bdivya66/run-your-first-python-build-using-jenkins-905dc581b8c4"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul \ulc4 \outl0\strokewidth-182 https://medium.com/@bdivya66/run-your-first-python-build-using-jenkins-905dc581b8c4}}\
\
Note the examples in the medium post are for windows. You won\'92t need to set a python path to run files, just move into the correct source directory and run \'93python3 <pythonfilename.py>\'94\
\
\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs36 \cf2 \outl0\strokewidth-112 Helpful Scripts\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs22 \cf2 \outl0\strokewidth-182 \
Two things you might like to do are to check if the python code in a directory compiles, or else, perhaps, run the API in a shell then figure out the process ID and delete it. Here are three quick scriptlets that might be helpful as build actions or post-build actions in Jenkins:\
\
\pard\tx20\tx360\pardeftab720\li360\fi-360\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\fs20\fsmilli10000 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1)	}
\fs22 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone \outl0\strokewidth-182 \strokec2 This code checks if the code In the current working directory (
\f6 \outl0\strokewidth-182 \'93
\f1 \outl0\strokewidth-182 .\'94) will compile:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3 \cf2 \outl0\strokewidth-182 python3 -m compileall -q .
\f1 \outl0\strokewidth-182 \
\
2) This code runs the stand up triangle app, a post-build step you define, then ends the triangle API:\

\f3 \outl0\strokewidth-182 \
python3 stand_up_triangle_api.py &\
\
#Your test post-build test scripts here\
\
# Find the PID(s) of the Python script\
PIDS=$(ps -ef | grep "stand_up_triangle_api.py" | grep -v grep | awk '\{print $2\}')\
\
# Kill all those PIDs\
if [ -n "$PIDS" ]; then\
  echo "Killing API process(es): $PIDS"\
  kill $PIDS\
else\
  echo "No API process found"\
fi\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1 \cf2 \outl0\strokewidth-182 \
3)  For debugging, you might want to print the working directory. The command for that is \'93pwd\'94}